q(92, "Besides statins and aspirin, the mortality-reducing group is:", ["ACE inhibitors/ARBs", "CCBs", "Nitrates", "Ivabradine"], "The mortality column lists ACE inhibitors/ARBs.")

unit("Ivabradine",
     "Ivabradine is described as an AHA bradycardiac drug: it blocks the funny (Na+/K+) channels carrying the If current, blocking the SA node to lower heart rate while contraction remains normal. FDA approved for chronic CHF, it is used off-label for stable angina, inappropriate sinus tachycardia and postural tachycardia syndrome. Adverse effects are a higher risk of atrial fibrillation (from increased action of the functional circuit) and visual phenomena from If channel block in the retina - luminous phenomena (multiple coloured halos) and phosphenes (images of light perceived with closed eyes).")
q(92, "Ivabradine is described as an:", ["AHA bradycardiac drug", "AHA tachycardiac drug", "Inotrope", "Diuretic"], "The heading line calls ivabradine an AHA bradycardiac drug.")
q(92, "Ivabradine blocks which channels?", ["Funny (Na+/K+) channels", "L-type Ca2+ channels", "Delayed rectifier K+ channels", "Late inward Na+ channels"], "The MOA line says blocks funny (Na+/K+) channels.")
q(92, "The current carried by the funny channels is abbreviated as:", ["If", "Ih only", "IK", "ICa"], "The MOA says funny channels (If current).")
q(92, "Blockade by ivabradine occurs at the:", ["SA node", "AV node", "Purkinje fibres", "Ventricular myocardium"], "The MOA arrow says block SA node.")
q(92, "The result of SA nodal blockade by ivabradine is:", ["Decreased heart rate", "Increased heart rate", "Decreased contractility", "Increased contractility"], "The first outcome is down heart rate.")
q(92, "Along with lowered heart rate, ivabradine preserves:", ["Normal contraction", "Increased contractility", "Decreased preload", "AV block"], "The second outcome is normal contraction.")
q(92, "The FDA-approved use of ivabradine is:", ["Chronic CHF", "Stable angina", "POTS", "Inappropriate sinus tachycardia"], "The FDA approved line says chronic CHF.")
q(92, "Which of the following is an off-label use of ivabradine?", ["Stable angina", "Chronic CHF", "Hypertension", "Atrial fibrillation"], "Stable angina is listed under off-label uses.")
q(92, "Ivabradine is used off-label in inappropriate sinus tachycardia and:", ["Postural tachycardia syndrome (POTS)", "Atrial flutter", "WPW syndrome", "Ventricular fibrillation"], "The off-label list includes postural tachycardia syndrome (POTS).")
q(92, "Ivabradine increases the risk of:", ["Atrial fibrillation", "Ventricular fibrillation", "Asystole", "Heart block"], "The S/E line says up R/o atrial fibrillation.")
q(92, "The increased atrial fibrillation risk with ivabradine is due to increased action of the:", ["Functional circuit", "Accessory pathway", "AV node", "Purkinje system"], "The S/E annotation says d/t up action of functional circuit.")
q(92, "Visual phenomena with ivabradine occur due to If channel block in the:", ["Retina", "Cornea", "Optic nerve", "Ciliary body"], "The S/E annotation says d/t If channel block in retina.")
q(92, "Luminous phenomena with ivabradine are described as:", ["Multiple coloured halos in the visual field", "Complete blindness", "Double vision", "Loss of colour vision"], "The luminous phenomena bullet describes multiple coloured halos in the visual field.")
q(92, "Phosphenes are described as:", ["Images of light perceived with closed eyes", "Flashes only on eye movement", "Halos around lights", "Blurring of near vision"], "The phosphenes bullet defines images of light perceived with closed eyes.")

unit("Ranolazine",
     "Ranolazine at normal dose blocks late inward sodium (LINA+) channels; at high dose it also blocks the delayed rectifier K+ channel, thereby blocking repolarization. Blocking late inward Na+ reduces intracellular Na+, which reduces Na+/Ca2+ exchanger activity, lowers intracellular Ca2+ in the myocardium and so lowers O2 demand, while BP and heart rate stay normal in a normal person. FDA approved for stable angina, it is used off-label in HOCM and ventricular tachycardia, and its notable effects are a reduced risk of atrial fibrillation and a fall in HbA1c that reverses insulin resistance.")
q(93, "At normal dose, ranolazine blocks:", ["Late inward Na+ (LINA+) channels", "Funny channels", "L-type Ca2+ channels", "ATP-sensitive K+ channels"], "The normal dose MOA line says blocks late inward Na+ (LINA+) channels.")
q(93, "At high dose, ranolazine blocks the:", ["Delayed rectifier K+ channel", "Rapidly activating K+ channel only", "Na+/K+ ATPase", "If channel"], "The high dose MOA line says blocks delayed rectifier K+ channel.")
q(93, "Blockade of the delayed rectifier K+ channel by ranolazine blocks:", ["Repolarization", "Depolarization", "Conduction", "Automaticity only"], "The high dose line ends: blocks repolarization.")
q(93, "In the ranolazine diagram, blockade of LINA+ channels leads to decreased:", ["Intracellular Na+", "Intracellular Ca2+ immediately", "Heart rate", "Blood pressure"], "The diagram shows down Na+ inside the cell as the first step.")
q(93, "The fall in intracellular Na+ reduces the activity of the:", ["Na+/Ca2+ exchanger", "Na+/K+ ATPase", "Na+/H+ antiporter", "Na+-glucose cotransporter"], "The diagram points from down Na+ to the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger.")
q(93, "Reduced Na+/Ca2+ exchanger activity lowers:", ["Intracellular Ca2+ in myocardium", "Intracellular Na+", "Extracellular Ca2+", "Sarcoplasmic Ca2+ release only"], "The next step is down intracellular Ca2+ in myocardium.")
q(93, "The final hemodynamic consequence of the ranolazine cascade is decreased:", ["O2 demand", "O2 supply", "Blood pressure", "Heart rate"], "The cascade ends at down O2 demand.")
q(93, "In a normal person, ranolazine maintains normal:", ["BP and HR", "BP only", "HR only", "Cardiac output only"], "The annotation says maintains normal BP and HR (in a normal person).")
q(93, "The FDA-approved use of ranolazine is:", ["Stable angina", "Chronic CHF", "Hypertension", "Arrhythmia"], "The FDA approved line says stable angina.")
q(93, "Ranolazine is used off-label in hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy and:", ["Ventricular tachycardia", "Atrial fibrillation", "Complete heart block", "Sinus bradycardia"], "The off-label list gives HOCM and ventricular tachycardia.")
q(93, "In ventricular tachycardia, ranolazine acts by decreasing Ca2+ in ventricle cells, thereby decreasing:", ["Excitability", "Conduction velocity", "Refractory period", "Contractility only"], "The off-label annotation reads down Ca2+ in ventricle cells, then down excitability.")
q(93, "Decreased ventricular excitability with ranolazine leads to decreased:", ["Heart rate", "Blood pressure", "Stroke volume", "Preload"], "The annotation chain ends at down HR.")
q(93, "Unlike ivabradine, ranolazine decreases the risk of:", ["Atrial fibrillation", "Ventricular tachycardia", "Heart failure", "Hypoglycemia"], "The S/E line says down risk of atrial fibrillation.")
q(93, "Ranolazine decreases HbA1C, which indicates it reverses:", ["Insulin resistance", "Insulin secretion", "Glucagon action", "Renal glucose loss"], "The S/E line says down HbA1C (reverses insulin resistance).")

unit("Other Antianginal Drugs: Nicorandil, Fasudil, Allopurinol, Bosentan and Trimetazidine",
     "The remaining antianginals act on distinct targets: nicorandil opens K+ channels and causes vasodilation; fasudil is a Rho-kinase inhibitor that causes vasodilation; allopurinol produces vasodilatation and is also used in gout; bosentan is used in pulmonary hypertension; and trimetazidine blocks partial fatty acid oxidase (pFox), inhibiting metabolism of triglycerides (the main source of ATP in the heart) while promoting glycolysis in the heart, so O2 demand falls.")
q(93, "Nicorandil acts as a:", ["K+ channel opener", "K+ channel blocker", "Rho-kinase inhibitor", "Na+ channel blocker"], "The nicorandil line says K+ channel opener.")
q(93, "The vascular effect of nicorandil is:", ["Vasodilation", "Vasoconstriction", "No vascular effect", "Selective venoconstriction"], "The nicorandil line ends with vasodilation.")
q(93, "Fasudil acts as a:", ["Rho-kinase inhibitor", "K+ channel opener", "pFox inhibitor", "Endothelin antagonist"], "The fasudil line says Rho-kinase inhibitor.")
q(93, "The vascular effect of fasudil is:", ["Vasodilation", "Vasoconstriction", "Increased cardiac output", "Bradycardia"], "The fasudil line ends with vasodilation.")
q(93, "Allopurinol is noted in the antianginal page for:", ["Vasodilatation, and is used in gout", "Coronary spasm", "Platelet inhibition", "Cholesterol lowering"], "The allopurinol line says d/t vasodilatation (used in Rx of gout).")
q(93, "Bosentan is used in:", ["Pulmonary hypertension", "Systemic hypertension", "HOCM", "Heart failure"], "The bosentan line says in pulmonary hypertension.")
q(93, "Trimetazidine blocks:", ["pFox (partial fatty acid oxidase)", "HMG-CoA reductase", "Rho kinase", "K+ channels"], "The trimetazidine line says blocks pFox (partial fatty acid oxidase).")
q(93, "Blocking pFox inhibits metabolism of triglycerides, which are the:", ["Source of ATP in heart", "Source of glucose in heart", "Cause of angina", "Cause of spasm"], "The parenthesis says TG: source of ATP in heart.")
q(93, "Along with inhibiting TG metabolism, trimetazidine promotes:", ["Glycolysis in heart", "Lipolysis in heart", "Gluconeogenesis", "Ketogenesis"], "The arrow says promotes glycolysis in heart.")
q(93, "The net effect of trimetazidine on the heart is:", ["Decreased O2 demand", "Increased O2 demand", "Increased heart rate", "Increased contractility"], "The trimetazidine chain ends at down O2 demand.")
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 25: Hypolipidemic Drugs (p94-100)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
start(25)
unit("Sources of Lipids and the Physiology Diagram",
     "Hypolipidemic drugs are used in atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD). The physiology page builds lipid handling from three sources: food entering the small intestine (cholesterol, triglycerides and bile acids, with ezetimibe blocking the NPC1L1 transporter and esterification by ACAT in the epithelial cell), storage in adipose tissue (where niacin blocks the adipocyte and hormone sensitive lipase releases free fatty acids in the fasting state), and synthesis in the liver. Enterohepatic circulation carries bile acids back through the duodenum; VLDL and chylomicrons reach the blood vessel, and the remnants of VLDL and chylomicrons are removed by the liver.")
q(94, 'Hypolipidemic drugs are used in:', ['Atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD)', 'Venous thromboembolism', 'Hypertensive emergency', 'Heart failure with reduced EF'], 'The subtitle says used in Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease (ASCVD).')
q(94, 'The first source of lipids in the diagram is:', ['Food', 'Adipose storage', 'Hepatic synthesis', 'Peripheral metabolism'], 'The diagram numbers source 1 as Food.')
q(94, 'The lipid components absorbed from food in the small intestine include cholesterol, bile acids and:', ['Triglycerides (TG)', 'Free fatty acids only', 'Ketone bodies', 'Phospholipids only'], 'The small intestine box lists cholesterol, TG and bile acids.')
q(94, 'Ezetimibe acts at the intestinal epithelial cell by blocking:', ['NPC1L1', 'ACAT', 'MTTP', 'HSL'], 'The ezetimibe pill symbol sits on the NPC1L1 transporter.')
q(94, 'NPC1L1 stands for:', ['Niemann-Pick C1-like receptor', 'Nuclear protein C1 ligand', 'Non-polar cholesterol 1 lipase', 'Niemann-Pick carrier 1'], 'The index defines NPC1L1 as Niemann-Pick C1-like receptor.')
q(94, 'Inside the epithelial cell, cholesterol and TG undergo esterification by:', ['ACAT', 'HSL', 'LPL', 'MTTP'], 'The epithelial cell line says esterification by ACAT.')
q(94, 'ACAT stands for:', ['Acyl Coenzyme A Transferase', 'Acetyl CoA Acetyl Transferase', 'Acyl Carnitine Acyl Transferase', 'Amino CoA Transaminase'], 'The index defines ACAT as Acyl Coenzyme A Transferase.')
q(94, 'Bile acids from the small intestine return to the liver through the:', ['Enterohepatic circulation', 'Portal lymphatics', 'Thoracic duct', 'Renal tubules'], 'The diagram labels the loop from liver to duodenum to intestine as enterohepatic circulation.')
q(94, 'Drugs acting on bile acids in the gut to block the enterohepatic circulation are:', ['Bile acid binding resins', 'Fibrates', 'Statins', 'PCSK9 inhibitors'], 'The bile acid binding resins pill symbol is placed on the bile acid pathway.')
q(94, 'The drug class that blocks MTTP in the liver is represented by:', ['Lomitapide', 'Evinacumab', 'Icosapent', 'Niacin'], 'The lomitapide pill symbol blocks the MTTP step in the liver diagram.')
q(94, 'MTTP stands for:', ['Microsomal Triglyceride Transport Protein', 'Mitochondrial Transfer Protein', 'Membrane Triglyceride Transporter Protein', 'Microsomal Trans Fatty acid Protein'], 'The index defines MTTP as microsomal Triglyceride Transport Protein.')
q(94, 'Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is defined in the index as:', ['Lipoprotein Lipase', 'Lipid Protein Ligand', 'Lysosomal Phospholipase', 'Low-density Protein Lipase'], 'The index lists LPL: Lipoprotein Lipase.')
q(94, 'AngPTL3 is described in the index as a physiological protein that:', ['Blocks LPL', 'Activates LPL', 'Synthesises LPL', 'Degrades HDL'], 'The index says AngPTL3 is a physiological protein blocking LPL.')
q(94, 'AngPTL3 stands for:', ['Angiopoietin Like Protein - 3', 'Angiotensin Like Peptide - 3', 'Angiotensinogen Precursor - 3', 'Angiogenic Lipid Protein - 3'], 'The index spells AngPTL3 as Angiopoietin Like Protein - 3.')
q(94, 'The monoclonal antibody that blocks AngPTL-3 is:', ['Evinacumab', 'Alirocumab', 'Evolocumab', 'Lomitapide'], 'The evinacumab pill symbol blocks AngPTL-3 on the diagram.')
q(94, 'The cholesterol content of VLDL is summarised in the index as VLDL:', ['TG & cholesterol', 'Cholesterol only', 'TG only', 'Phospholipid & cholesterol'], 'The index says VLDL: TG & cholesterol.')
q(94, 'The second source of lipids is the storage form in:', ['Adipose tissue', 'Bone marrow', 'Skeletal muscle', 'Kidney'], 'Source 2 is labelled storage form in adipose tissue.')
q(94, 'In adipose tissue, the drug shown blocking the adipocyte pathway is:', ['Niacin', 'Fibrates', 'Ezetimibe', 'Bempedoic acid'], 'The niacin pill symbol blocks the adipocyte pathway.')
q(94, 'In the fasting state, adipocytes release free fatty acids through the action of:', ['Hormone sensitive lipase (HSL)', 'Lipoprotein lipase (LPL)', 'ACAT', 'MTTP'], 'The diagram labels fasting state then HSL.')
q(94, 'HSL stands for:', ['Hormone Sensitive Lipase', 'Hepatic Steroid Lipase', 'High Solubility Lipase', 'Hydroxy Sterol Lipase'], 'The index defines HSL as Hormone Sensitive Lipase.')
q(94, 'Free fatty acids released from adipose tissue are used to form TG and:', ['ATP', 'Cholesterol', 'Bile acids', 'Ketones only'], 'The adipose branch shows FFA splitting to TG and ATP.')
q(94, 'The third source of lipids is synthesis in the:', ['Liver', 'Brain', 'Kidney', 'Adipocyte'], 'Source 3 is labelled synthesis in liver.')
q(94, 'In the liver diagram, proteins together with bile acid and TG are used for:', ['VLDL synthesis', 'Chylomicron storage', 'LDL receptor synthesis', 'Bile duct secretion'], 'The liver box labels proteins with VLDL synthesis.')
q(94, 'Chylomicrons and VLDL formed from the intestine and liver are shown entering the:', ['Blood vessel', 'Bile duct', 'Lymph node', 'Kidney'], 'The diagram arrow leads to the blood vessel containing VLDL and cm.')
q(94, 'The remnants of VLDL and chylomicrons are:', ['Removed by liver', 'Removed by kidney', 'Excreted in urine', 'Converted to bile acids directly'], 'The bottom arrow of the diagram says remnants of VLDL and Cm are removed by liver.')
q(94, 'Removal of VLDL and chylomicron remnants by the liver leads to decreased:', ['VLDL', 'HDL', 'LDL particles', 'ATP'], 'The first outcome of hepatic removal is down VLDL.')
q(94, 'Removal of remnants also decreases:', ['Chylomicrons', 'LDL particles', 'Bile acids', 'ATP'], 'The second outcome is down chylomicrons.')
q(94, 'Removal of remnants decreases TG and:', ['HDL', 'LDL particles', 'Bile acids', 'Cholesterol synthesis'], 'The diagram shows down TG, down HDL, and finally down LDL-particles.')
q(94, 'A drug symbol placed on the blood vessel/liver cell that increases lipolysis activity is:', ['Fibrates', 'Ezetimibe', 'Niacin', 'Bempedoic acid'], 'The fibrates pill symbol is placed next to lipolysis activity.')
q(94, 'In the liver cell diagram, cholesterol plus TG is directed to storage or to:', ['Generate ATP for heart', 'Form bile salts', 'Form ketone bodies', 'Excrete in urine'], 'The liver cell shows storage (breast/adipose) and generate ATP (heart).')

unit("Cholesterol Synthesis, LDL Receptor Regulation and PCSK9 Drugs",
     "When a cell requires cholesterol it makes it from citrate: ATP citrate lyase converts citrate to acetyl CoA (blocked by bempedoic acid), HMG CoA reductase converts HMG CoA to mevalonic acid (blocked by statins) and the pathway ends in cholesterol for bile acids and steroids. The same cholesterol need upregulates synthesis of the LDL receptor - the effect shared by statins, bempedoic acid, ezetimibe and bile acid binding resins. Plasma LDL reaching the cell is degraded to cholesterol, PCSK9 degrades LDL receptors, alirocumab and evolocumab are monoclonal antibodies against PCSK9, and inclisiran is a siRNA that blocks synthesis of PCSK9. Evinacumab blocks AngPTL-3 to block LPL and lower LDL cholesterol in FHC, while lomitapide is also used in familial hypercholesterolemia.")
q(95, 'The cholesterol synthesis pathway begins when citrate is converted to acetyl CoA by:', ['ATP citrate lyase', 'HMG CoA reductase', 'ACAT', 'LPL'], 'The diagram labels ATP citrate lyase between citrate and acetyl CoA.')
q(95, 'ATP citrate lyase is blocked by:', ['Bempedoic acid', 'Statins', 'Ezetimibe', 'Niacin'], 'The bempedoic acid pill symbol blocks the ATP citrate lyase step.')
q(95, 'In the synthetic cascade, acetyl CoA is converted to:', ['HMG CoA', 'Mevalonic acid', 'Cholesterol', 'Bile acids'], 'The cascade goes acetyl CoA to HMG CoA.')
q(95, 'HMG CoA is converted to mevalonic acid by:', ['HMG CoA reductase', 'ATP citrate lyase', 'Lipoprotein lipase', 'ACAT'], 'The enzyme label between HMG CoA and mevalonic acid is HMG CoA reductase.')
q(95, 'HMG CoA reductase is blocked by:', ['Statins', 'Bempedoic acid', 'Bile acid binding resins', 'Ezetimibe'], 'The statin pill symbol blocks HMG CoA reductase.')
q(95, 'The end products of cholesterol in this pathway are bile acids and:', ['Steroids', 'Ketone bodies', 'ATP', 'Bile pigments'], 'The cascade ends at cholesterol then bile acids and steroids.')
q(95, 'Which four groups are shown upregulating synthesis of the LDL receptor?', ['Statins, bempedoic acid, ezetimibe, bile acid binding resins', 'Statins, fibrates, niacin, icosapent', 'Ezetimibe, PCSK9 inhibitors, inclisiran, evinacumab', 'Bempedoic acid, niacin, fibrates, resins'], 'The (+) bracket on the diagram lists all four drug groups upregulating LDL receptor synthesis.')
q(95, 'Plasma LDL entering the cell is degraded to:', ['Cholesterol', 'Bile acids', 'Mevalonic acid', 'TG'], 'The diagram labels plasma LDL then degrades then cholesterol.')
q(95, 'PCSK9 acts on the LDL receptor by:', ['Promoting its degradation', 'Increasing its synthesis', 'Blocking its recycling only', 'Converting it to HDL'], 'The diagram ties PCSK9 to degradation of the LDL receptor.')
q(95, 'The monoclonal antibodies that block PCSK9 are:', ['Alirocumab and evolocumab', 'Evinacumab and lomitapide', 'Ezetimibe and bempedoic acid', 'Inclisiran and icosapent'], 'The diagram names alirocumab and evolocumab blocking PCSK9.')
q(95, 'Inclisiran blocks the synthesis of:', ['PCSK9', 'LDL receptor', 'HMG CoA reductase', 'AngPTL-3'], 'The inclisiran arrow is labelled blocks synthesis, pointing to PCSK9.')
q(95, 'Inclisiran belongs to which drug class?', ['Small interfering RNA antagonists', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'Bile acid sequestrants', 'Oral PPAR agonists'], 'The drug list on p96 labels inclisiran as small interfering RNA antagonists.')
q(95, 'Evinacumab blocks AngPTL-3, which leads to blockade of:', ['LPL', 'PCSK9', 'HMG CoA reductase', 'NPC1L1'], 'The note says evinacumab blocks AngPTL-3, which blocks LPL.')
q(95, 'The net effect of evinacumab is decreased:', ['LDL cholesterol', 'HDL cholesterol', 'Triglycerides only', 'Lp(a)'], 'The evinacumab note ends with down LDL cholesterol.')
q(95, 'Evinacumab is used in the treatment of:', ['Familial hypercholesterolemia (FHC)', 'Familial hypertriglyceridemia', 'Type III hyperlipoproteinemia', 'Gout'], 'The evinacumab note says Rx of FHC.')
q(95, 'Lomitapide is used for:', ['Familial hypercholesterolemia (FHC)', 'Familial hypertriglyceridemia', 'Chylomicronemia syndrome', 'Nephrotic syndrome'], 'The lomitapide note says Rx of familial hypercholesterolemia (FHC).')
q(95, 'The classification of hypolipidemic drugs based on action divides them into those lowering lipoprotein A, LDL and:', ['TG', 'HDL', 'ATP', 'Bile acids'], 'The classification branches are down Lp(a), down TG and down LDL.')

unit("Niacin: the Lipoprotein-A Lowering Drug",
     "Niacin is highly toxic and therefore not preferred. It lowers free fatty acids, which lowers TG, VLDL and finally LDL; it is used to lower Lp(a) along with statin plus ezetimibe therapy and gives the maximum increase in HDL (more than statin) although this indication is obsolete. Its side effects are potent hepatotoxicity, insulin resistance progressing to diabetes mellitus and flushing caused by prostaglandin release with vasodilation, for which aspirin is the treatment of choice because it blocks cyclooxygenase and reduces prostaglandin synthesis.")
q(95, 'Niacin is described as:', ['Highly toxic and not preferred', 'Safest hypolipidemic', 'First-line for LDL', 'DOC in pregnancy'], 'The niacin line says highly toxic and not preferred.')
q(95, 'The first step of niacin action is decreased:', ['FFA', 'TG', 'VLDL', 'LDL'], 'The niacin MOA chain starts with down FFA.')
q(95, 'Niacin lowers TG, which lowers VLDL and finally:', ['LDL', 'HDL', 'Lp(a)', 'Bile acids'], 'The chain reads down FFA, down TG, down VLDL, down LDL.')
q(95, 'Niacin is the drug used to lower:', ['Lipoprotein A (Lp-a)', 'Triglycerides only', 'LDL only', 'Bile acids'], 'Niacin is grouped under the down lipoprotein-A branch.')
q(95, 'Niacin is used for Lp(a) lowering along with:', ['Statins and ezetimibe', 'Fibrates and icosapent', 'Resins and niacin', 'PCSK9 inhibitors alone'], 'The niacin use line says down Lp-a with Rx of statins + ezetimibe.')
q(95, 'Niacin produces the maximum increase in:', ['HDL', 'LDL', 'VLDL', 'Lp(a)'], 'The use line says up HDL (obsolete): maximum.')
q(95, 'The maximum HDL-raising effect is seen with:', ['Niacin > statin', 'Statin > niacin', 'Fibrates > niacin', 'Resins > statin'], 'The niacin use line specifies maximum (niacin > statin).')
q(95, 'The HDL-raising use of niacin is labelled:', ['Obsolete', 'First line', 'Life saving', 'DOC in pregnancy'], 'The HDL use is annotated obsolete.')
q(95, 'The most serious toxicity of niacin is:', ['Potent hepatotoxicity', 'Nephrotoxicity', 'Ototoxicity', 'Bone marrow suppression'], 'The side effect list begins with potent hepatotoxicity.')
q(95, 'Niacin causes insulin resistance leading to:', ['Diabetes mellitus', 'Hypoglycemia', 'Diabetic ketoacidosis', 'Insulinoma'], 'The side effect line says insulin resistance to diabetes mellitus.')
q(95, 'Flushing with niacin is caused by increased release of:', ['Prostaglandins (PGs)', 'Histamine', 'Bradykinin', 'Substance P'], 'The flushing mechanism says up release of prostaglandins.')
q(95, 'Prostaglandin release in niacin flushing causes:', ['Vasodilation', 'Vasoconstriction', 'Bronchoconstriction', 'Bradycardia'], 'The flushing mechanism ends in vasodilation.')
q(95, 'The DOC for niacin-induced flushing is:', ['Aspirin', 'Paracetamol', 'Ibuprofen only', 'Ondansetron'], 'The flushing line says DOC for Rx: aspirin.')
q(95, 'Aspirin relieves niacin flushing by blocking:', ['Cyclooxygenase', 'Lipoxygenase', 'Phospholipase A2', 'Prostaglandin receptors'], 'The annotation says aspirin blocks cyclooxygenase.')
q(95, 'Blocking cyclooxygenase with aspirin decreases the synthesis of:', ['PGs (prostaglandins)', 'Leukotrienes', 'Thromboxane only', 'Bile acids'], 'The chain ends at down synthesis of PGs.')
unit("Triglyceride-lowering Drugs: Omega-3 Fatty Acids, Icosapent and Fibrates",
     "The TG-lowering group contains omega-3 fatty acids, icosapent and fibrates, with fibrates split into halogenated (clofibrate, bezafibrate, fenofibrate) and non-halogenated (gemfibrozil) agents. Icosapent is a derivative of omega-3 fatty acids that blocks release of VLDL by the liver and is used in familial hypertriglyceridemia. Fibrates stimulate PPAR-alpha, which increases LPL synthesis in capillary endothelium, lowering chylomicrons, VLDL, TG and LDL particles; they are DOC in hypertriglyceridemia, chylomicronemia syndrome and type III hyperlipoproteinemia, and fenofibrate also lowers uric acid like febuxostat. Their side effects are myopathy and gall stones, maximal with clofibrate.")
q(96, 'The drugs listed under decreased triglycerides are omega-3 fatty acids, fibrates and:', ['Icosapent', 'Ezetimibe', 'Niacin', 'Bempedoic acid'], 'The TG branch lists omega-3 fatty acids, icosapent and fibrates.')
q(96, 'Fibrates are divided into halogenated and:', ['Non-halogenated', 'Synthetic', 'Lipophilic', 'Renally excreted'], 'The fibrate branch splits into halogenated and non-halogenated.')
q(96, 'Which of the following is a halogenated fibrate?', ['Clofibrate', 'Gemfibrozil', 'Fenofibrate only', 'Bezafibrate only'], 'The halogenated list gives clofibrate, bezafibrate and fenofibrate.')
q(96, 'The non-halogenated fibrate listed is:', ['Gemfibrozil', 'Clofibrate', 'Bezafibrate', 'Fenofibrate'], 'The non-halogenated branch gives gemfibrozil.')
q(96, 'Icosapent is a derivative of:', ['Omega-3 fatty acids', 'Omega-6 fatty acids', 'Bile acids', 'Statins'], 'The icosapent line says derivative of omega-3 fatty acids.')
q(96, 'The mechanism of icosapent is blockade of the release of:', ['VLDL by liver', 'LDL by liver', 'Chylomicrons by intestine', 'Bile acids by gallbladder'], 'The icosapent MOA says blocks release of VLDL by liver.')
q(96, 'The use of icosapent is:', ['Rx of familial hypertriglyceridemia', 'Rx of familial hypercholesterolemia', 'Rx of nephrotic syndrome', 'Rx of gout'], 'The icosapent use is Rx of familial hypertriglyceridemia.')
q(96, 'Fibrates act by stimulating:', ['PPAR-alpha', 'PPAR-gamma', 'PCSK9', 'NPC1L1'], 'The fibrate MOA starts with stimulate PPAR-alpha.')
q(96, 'PPAR-alpha stands for:', ['Peroxisome Proliferator Activated Receptor-alpha', 'Protein Phosphorylation Activated Receptor-alpha', 'Peripheral Protein Absorption Receptor-alpha', 'Peroxisomal Protein Anabolic Receptor-alpha'], 'The page expands PPAR-alpha as Peroxisome Proliferator Activated Receptor-alpha.')
q(96, 'Stimulation of PPAR-alpha increases synthesis of LPL in the:', ['Capillary endothelium', 'Hepatocyte cytosol', 'Adipocyte nucleus', 'Renal tubule'], 'The MOA arrow says up LPL synthesis in capillary endothelium.')
q(96, 'Fibrates decrease chylomicrons, TG and:', ['VLDL', 'HDL', 'Bile acids', 'ATP'], 'The outcome branch lists down chylomicrons, down VLDL, down TG.')
q(96, 'The effect of fibrates on LDL is described as:', ['Insignificant', 'Marked', 'Maximum', 'Absent'], 'The fibrate branch says down LDL (insignificant).')
q(96, 'Fibrates decrease LDL particles (LDL-P) while LDL cholesterol:', ['Increases slightly', 'Decreases markedly', 'Stays unchanged', 'Doubles'], 'The MOA branch says down LDL particles (LDL-P) and up LDL cholesterol slightly.')
q(96, 'A listed side effect of fibrates is:', ['Myopathy', 'Ototoxicity', 'Optic neuritis', 'Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus'], 'The S/E list gives myopathy and gall stones.')
q(96, 'Gall stones (cholelithiasis) are maximum with which fibrate?', ['Clofibrate', 'Gemfibrozil', 'Fenofibrate', 'Bezafibrate'], 'The S/E line says gall stones maximum with clofibrate.')
q(96, 'Fibrates are DOC in hypertriglyceridemia, type III hyperlipoproteinemia and:', ['Chylomicronemia syndrome', 'Familial hypercholesterolemia', 'Nephrotic syndrome', 'Metabolic syndrome only'], 'The uses list gives hypertriglyceridemia, chylomicronemia syndrome and type III hyperlipoproteinemia.')
q(96, 'The fibrate used for gout is:', ['Fenofibrate', 'Gemfibrozil', 'Clofibrate', 'Bezafibrate'], 'The use line says Rx of gout: fenofibrate.')
q(96, 'Fenofibrate acts on uric acid similar to which drug?', ['Febuxostat', 'Allopurinol', 'Colchicine', 'Probenecid'], 'The gout line says similar to febuxostat.')
q(96, 'The uric acid effect of fenofibrate is to:', ['Decrease uric acid', 'Increase uric acid', 'Have no effect', 'Increase urate stones'], 'The gout line ends with down uric acid.')
q(96, 'Besides fibrates, the other drug noted to cause cholelithiasis is:', ['Octreotide', 'Ondansetron', 'Omeprazole', 'Olanzapine'], 'The note says drugs causing cholelithiasis: fibrates, octreotide.')

unit("LDL-lowering Drugs: Statins, Bempedoic Acid, Ezetimibe, PCSK9 Inhibitors, Inclisiran and Resins",
     "The LDL-lowering indications are ASCVD such as angina, myocardial infarction and stroke, and any patient with raised LDL. The drugs are statins (the DOC, marked as the affordable oral drug class), bempedoic acid (affordable oral drug), ezetimibe (blocks NPC1L1 to reduce cholesterol absorption with upregulation of LDL receptors to lower plasma LDL), PCSK9 inhibitors given as intravenous monoclonal antibodies (evolocumab, alirocumab), inclisiran (small interfering RNA antagonists that block synthesis of PCSK9) and bile acid binding resins.")
q(96, 'The LDL-lowering indication list under ASCVD includes angina, myocardial infarction and:', ['Stroke', 'Gout', 'Retinopathy', 'Nephropathy'], 'The indication line lists angina/myocardial infarction/stroke.')
q(96, 'The other LDL-lowering indication is patients with:', ['Raised LDL', 'Low LDL', 'Raised HDL', 'Low TG'], 'The second indication bullet says in patients with up LDL.')
q(96, 'The DOC among LDL-lowering drugs is:', ['Statins', 'PCSK9 inhibitors', 'Inclisiran', 'Bile acid binding resins'], 'The drug flowchart marks statins (DOC).')
q(96, 'Bempedoic acid is described as:', ['Affordable oral drug', 'Intravenous antibody', 'siRNA', 'Bile acid sequestrant'], 'The bempedoic acid label says affordable oral drug.')
q(96, 'Ezetimibe lowers plasma LDL by first blocking:', ['NPC1L1', 'PCSK9', 'HMG CoA reductase', 'MTTP'], 'The ezetimibe box says blocks NPC1L1.')
q(96, 'Blocking NPC1L1 with ezetimibe decreases:', ['Cholesterol absorption', 'Cholesterol synthesis', 'Bile acid reabsorption', 'TG synthesis'], 'The ezetimibe flow says down cholesterol absorption.')
q(96, 'The step after decreased cholesterol absorption with ezetimibe is:', ['Upregulation of LDL receptors', 'Downregulation of LDL receptors', 'Blockade of PCSK9', 'Blockade of LPL'], 'The ezetimibe flow says upregulate LDL receptors.')
q(96, 'The final effect of ezetimibe in the flowchart is decreased:', ['Plasma LDL', 'Plasma HDL', 'Plasma TG only', 'Bile acids'], 'The ezetimibe chain ends at down plasma LDL.')
q(96, 'The PCSK9 inhibitors in the flowchart are given as:', ['I/v monoclonal antibodies', 'Oral tablets', 'Subcutaneous siRNA', 'Inhaled powder'], 'The PCSK9 inhibitor column says i/v monoclonal antibodies.')
q(96, 'The two PCSK9 monoclonal antibodies named are:', ['Evolocumab and alirocumab', 'Evinacumab and lomitapide', 'Ezetimibe and bempedoic acid', 'Inclisiran and icosapent'], 'The PCSK9 column names evolocumab and alirocumab.')
q(96, 'Inclisiran is classified as:', ['Small interfering RNA antagonists', 'Monoclonal antibody', 'Bile acid sequestrant', 'Fibrate'], 'The inclisiran column says small interfering RNA antagonists.')
q(96, 'Inclisiran acts by blocking synthesis of:', ['PCSK9', 'LDL receptor', 'NPC1L1', 'LPL'], 'The inclisiran column says block synthesis of PCSK9.')

unit("Bile Acid Binding Resins",
     "Bile acid binding resins are cholestyramine, colesevelam (preferred because of fewer side effects) and colestipol. They inhibit the enterohepatic circulation, so bile acid in the liver falls, cholesterol synthesis rises, demand for cholesterol and LDL rises, LDL receptors increase and plasma LDL finally falls. Their side effects are GI upset (diarrhea/bloating), hypertriglyceridemia, hyperchloremic alkalosis, reduced fat-soluble vitamins E/D/A/K and reduced absorption of other drugs. Uniquely among hypolipidemics they are safe and efficacious as DOC in pregnancy and children, because all other hypolipidemics are contraindicated in pregnancy.")
q(97, 'The bile acid binding resins listed are cholestyramine, colestipol and:', ['Colesevelam', 'Colchicine', 'Colestilan only', 'Colecalciferol'], 'The drug list gives cholestyramine, colesevelam and colestipol.')
q(97, 'Which resin is preferred and why?', ['Colesevelam, due to less side effects', 'Cholestyramine, due to cost', 'Colestipol, due to potency', 'Colesevelam, due to IV route'], 'The drug list marks colesevelam preferred due to less s/e.')
q(97, 'The mechanism of resins begins by inhibiting the:', ['Enterohepatic circulation', 'Cholesterol synthesis pathway', 'LPL activity', 'PCSK9 synthesis'], 'The MOA line says inhibit enterohepatic circulation.')
q(97, 'Inhibition of enterohepatic circulation by resins first decreases:', ['Bile acid in liver', 'Cholesterol in liver', 'LDL receptors', 'Plasma LDL'], 'The MOA cascade begins down bile acid in liver.')
q(97, 'After bile acid falls, the cascade raises synthesis of:', ['Cholesterol', 'Bile acids', 'Triglycerides', 'ATP'], 'The next step in the cascade is up synthesis of cholesterol.')
q(97, 'The resin cascade proceeds to increased demand for cholesterol and then increased demand for:', ['LDL', 'HDL', 'VLDL', 'TG'], 'The cascade says up demand for cholesterol then up demand for LDL.')
q(97, 'The step after increased LDL demand is increased:', ['LDL receptors', 'LDL particles', 'Bile acid reabsorption', 'LPL activity'], 'The cascade continues up LDL receptors.')
q(97, 'The final step of the resin MOA is decreased:', ['Plasma LDL', 'Plasma HDL', 'Bile acid excretion', 'Cholesterol synthesis'], 'The cascade ends at down plasma LDL.')
q(97, 'A listed GI side effect of resins is:', ['Diarrhea/bloating', 'Constipation only', 'Hematemesis', 'Melena'], 'The S/E line says GI upset: diarrhea/bloating.')
q(97, 'Resins cause which lipid abnormality as a side effect?', ['Hypertriglyceridemia', 'Hypotriglyceridemia', 'Hypercholesterolemia', 'Low HDL'], 'The S/E list includes hypertriglyceridemia.')
q(97, 'The acid-base side effect of resins is:', ['Hyperchloremic alkalosis', 'Metabolic acidosis', 'Respiratory acidosis', 'Hypernatremic acidosis'], 'The S/E list includes hyperchloremic alkalosis.')
q(97, 'Resins decrease which fat-soluble vitamins?', ['E/D/A/K', 'B1/B6/B12', 'C and folate', 'None'], 'The S/E list gives down vitamin E/D/A/K (fat soluble vitamins).')
q(97, 'A clinically important interaction of resins is decreased:', ['Absorption of other drugs', 'Absorption of water', 'Hepatic metabolism', 'Renal excretion'], 'The S/E list includes down absorption of other drugs.')
q(97, 'Resins are DOC in pregnancy and:', ['Children', 'Elderly', 'Diabetics', 'Renal failure'], 'The uses line says DOC in pregnancy and children.')
q(97, 'Resins are described in pregnancy and children as:', ['Safe and efficacious', 'Contraindicated', 'Second line', 'Not studied'], 'The uses line says safe and efficacious.')
q(97, 'Regarding pregnancy, how many hypolipidemics are contraindicated?', ['All except bile acid binding resins', 'None', 'Only statins', 'Only fibrates'], 'The note box says all hypolipidemics C/I in pregnancy except bile acid binding resins.')
q(97, 'The maximum LDL reduction of 70% is achieved by:', ['PCSK9 inhibitors', 'Statins', 'Inclisiran', 'Others'], 'The note ranks PCSK9 inhibitors (70%) highest.')
q(97, 'According to the same note, statins lower LDL by about:', ['60%', '70%', '50%', '15-25%'], 'The ranking gives statins 60%.')
q(97, 'Inclisiran lowers LDL by about:', ['50%', '60%', '70%', '15-25%'], 'The ranking gives inclisiran 50%.')
q(97, 'Other hypolipidemic drugs lower LDL by about:', ['15-25%', '30-40%', '50%', '70%'], 'The ranking ends with others (15-25%).')
q(97, 'Preference for prescription favours statins over PCSK9 inhibitors because statins are:', ['Oral and low cost', 'Not oral and low cost', 'IV and high cost', 'Oral and high cost'], 'The preference note says statins (oral, low cost) over PCSK9 inhibitors (not oral, high cost).')

unit("Statins: Mechanism of Action and Pleiotropic Effects",
     "Statins save lives by blocking HMG-CoA reductase, an enzyme with maximum activity at night; the resulting effects are lowered LDL, VLDL and TG along with raised HDL and Lp-alpha. Beyond lipid lowering, statins have pleiotropic (non-hypolipidemic) effects: they are anti-aggregant, anti-coagulant, vasodilators through increased release of nitric oxide, antioxidants, anti-inflammatory and they stabilise the atherosclerotic plaque.")
q(97, 'The life-saving hypolipidemic effect of statins is mediated by blocking:', ['HMG-CoA reductase', 'ATP citrate lyase', 'Lipoprotein lipase', 'ACAT'], 'The statin MOA says block HMG-CoA reductase.')
q(97, 'HMG-CoA reductase shows maximum activity at:', ['Night', 'Morning', 'Midday', 'All times equally'], 'The statin MOA annotates maximum activity at night.')
q(97, 'The lipid effects of statins include decreased LDL, VLDL and:', ['TG', 'HDL', 'Lp-alpha', 'Bile acids'], 'The statin arrow lists down LDL, down VLDL, down TG.')
q(97, 'Statins increase HDL and:', ['Lp-alpha', 'LDL particles', 'Chylomicrons', 'Bile acids'], 'The arrow pair lists up HDL, up Lp-alpha.')
q(97, 'Pleiotropic effects of statins are also called:', ['Non-hypolipidemic effects', 'Hypolipidemic effects', 'Anticoagulant-only effects', 'Diuretic effects'], 'The section is titled pleiotropic (non-hypolipidemic) effects.')
q(97, 'The vasodilator effect of statins is due to increased release of:', ['Nitric oxide', 'Prostacyclin only', 'Bradykinin', 'Histamine'], 'The pleiotropic list says vasodilator d/t up release of nitric oxide.')
q(97, 'Which of the following is a pleiotropic effect of statins on platelets?', ['Anti-aggregant effect', 'Pro-aggregant effect', 'Thrombocytopenia', 'Platelet storage defect'], 'The pleiotropic list begins with anti-aggregant.')
q(97, 'The coagulation-related pleiotropic effect of statins is:', ['Anti-coagulant effect', 'Pro-coagulant effect', 'Fibrinolytic block', 'Vitamin K antagonism'], 'The pleiotropic list includes anti-coagulant.')
q(97, 'Besides being an antioxidant, the other anti-inflammatory-related pleiotropic effect is:', ['Stabilisation of atherosclerotic plaque', 'Plaque rupture', 'Plaque calcification', 'Plaque haemorrhage'], 'The pleiotropic list ends with atherosclerotic plaque stabilization.')
q(97, 'The pleiotropic effect of statins opposing free radicals is:', ['Antioxidant effect', 'Oxidant effect', 'Lipid peroxidation', 'Xanthine oxidase activation'], 'Antioxidant is listed among the pleiotropic effects.')
unit("Statin Uses and Pharmacokinetics",
     "Statins are DOC in type II hyperlipoproteinemia and are used for ASCVD prophylaxis: primary prophylaxis in those with no prior attack but raised LDL/DM or a history of familial hypercholesterolemia, and secondary prophylaxis when there is a history of angina, MI or stroke. They are absorbed orally and taken up by the liver through OATP1B1; OATP1B1 is blocked by gemfibrozil and clarithromycin, producing statin toxicity, and clarithromycin also blocks glucuronidation and CYP3A4. All statins are CYP3A4 substrates except fluvastatin and rosuvastatin (CYP2C9) and pravastatin, which is excreted unchanged, is best in liver failure and is DOC for protease inhibitor induced dyslipidemia.")
q(98, 'The DOC use of statins is:', ['Type II hyperlipoproteinemia', 'Type I hyperlipoproteinemia', 'Type III hyperlipoproteinemia', 'Chylomicronemia syndrome'], 'The uses line says DOC: Type II hyperlipoproteinemia.')
q(98, 'Primary ASCVD prophylaxis with statins applies to patients with no prior attack but:', ['Raised risk due to up LDL/DM', 'Prior stroke', 'Prior angina', 'Prior MI'], 'The primary prophylaxis bullet says no h/o prior attack but up risk of up LDL/DM.')
q(98, 'Primary prophylaxis also covers a history of:', ['Familial hypercholesterolemia', 'Familial hypertriglyceridemia', 'Nephrotic syndrome', 'Gout'], 'The primary bullet lists h/o familial hypercholesterolemia.')
q(98, 'Secondary ASCVD prophylaxis with statins applies when there is a history of angina, MI or:', ['Stroke', 'Gout', 'Retinopathy', 'Nephropathy'], 'The secondary bullet says h/o angina/MI/stroke present.')
q(98, 'The route of administration of statins is:', ['Oral with good absorption', 'IV only', 'Subcutaneous', 'Transdermal'], 'The absorption line says oral (good absorption).')
q(98, 'Hepatic uptake of statins is mediated by:', ['OATP1B1', 'NPC1L1', 'PCSK9', 'MTTP'], 'The absorption line says hepatic uptake by OATP1B1.')
q(98, 'OATP1B1 stands for:', ['Organic anion transporter', 'Organic cation transporter', 'Oligopeptide transporter', 'Osmotic ATP pump'], 'The parenthesis expands OATP1B1 as organic anion transporter.')
q(98, 'Hepatic uptake of statins affects cholesterol synthesis in the:', ['Liver', 'Intestine', 'Adipocyte', 'Kidney'], 'The sub-bullet says affects cholesterol synthesis in liver.')
q(98, 'The two drugs that block OATP1B1 and produce statin toxicity are:', ['Gemfibrozil and clarithromycin', 'Fenofibrate and azithromycin', 'Ezetimibe and erythromycin', 'Icosapent and doxycycline'], 'The note lists OATP1B1 blocked by gemfibrozil and clarithromycin.')
q(98, 'Blockade of OATP1B1 leads to:', ['Statin toxicity', 'Reduced statin levels', 'Increased statin efficacy', 'Reduced myopathy risk'], 'The note says blockade leads to statin toxicity.')
q(98, 'Clarithromycin increases statin toxicity by blocking glucuronidation and:', ['CYP3A4', 'CYP2C9', 'OATP1B1 only', 'P-glycoprotein only'], 'The note lists clarithromycin blocking glucuronidation and CYP3A4.')
q(98, 'Phase I metabolism of statins occurs through CYP3A4 for:', ['All statins except fluvastatin, rosuvastatin and pravastatin', 'All statins without exception', 'Only lipophilic statins', 'Only hydrophilic statins'], 'The phase I line says all statins are metabolized by CYP3A4 except those listed.')
q(98, 'Fluvastatin and rosuvastatin are metabolized by:', ['CYP2C9', 'CYP3A4', 'CYP2D6', 'CYP1A2'], 'The bracket says fluvastatin and rosuvastatin are metabolized by CYP2C9.')
q(98, 'Pravastatin is handled by:', ['Excretion unchanged', 'CYP3A4 metabolism', 'CYP2C9 metabolism', 'Glucuronidation only'], 'The pravastatin line says excreted unchanged.')
q(98, 'Statins safe to use with CYP3A4 inhibitors are:', ['Fluvastatin, rosuvastatin and pravastatin', 'Simvastatin and atorvastatin', 'Lovastatin only', 'All statins'], 'The bracket labels the non-CYP3A4 statins as safe with CYP3A4 inhibitors.')
q(98, 'The statin best in liver failure is pravastatin because it is the only statin not metabolized by:', ['CYP450', 'Glucuronidation', 'Esterases', 'Renal tubules'], 'The annotation says best in liver failure (only statin not metabolized by CYP450).')
q(98, 'Pravastatin is DOC for dyslipidemia induced by:', ['Protease inhibitors', 'Thiazides', 'Beta blockers', 'Glucocorticoids'], 'The line says DOC for protease inhibitors induced dyslipidemia.')
q(98, 'Protease inhibitor anti-HIV drugs cause toxicity because they:', ['Inhibit enzymes', 'Induce enzymes', 'Block renal excretion', 'Block bile secretion'], 'The parenthesis says anti-HIV drug inhibiting enzymes causes toxicity.')
q(98, 'Phase II metabolism of statins is by:', ['Glucuronidation', 'Sulfation', 'Acetylation', 'Methylation'], 'Phase II line says glucuronidation.')
q(98, 'The statin with the maximum T1/2 is:', ['Rosuvastatin', 'Atorvastatin', 'Pitavastatin', 'Fluvastatin'], 'Point 2 says maximum T1/2: rosuvastatin > atorvastatin.')
q(98, 'Night time dosing of statins is based on maximum activity of the enzyme:', ['HMG CoA reductase', 'ATP citrate lyase', 'LPL', 'ACAT'], 'Point 3 says night dosing for maximum activity of HMGCoA enzyme.')
q(98, 'Night time dosing is not mandatory with:', ['Rosuvastatin and atorvastatin', 'Simvastatin and pravastatin', 'Fluvastatin and lovastatin', 'All statins'], 'Point 3 says not mandatory with rosuvastatin/atorvastatin.')
q(98, 'The most potent statin listed is:', ['Pitavastatin', 'Rosuvastatin', 'Atorvastatin', 'Simvastatin'], 'Point 4 says most potent: pitavastatin > rosuvastatin.')
q(98, 'The statin with maximum response is:', ['Rosuvastatin', 'Pitavastatin', 'Atorvastatin', 'Fluvastatin'], 'Point 5 says maximum response: rosuvastatin > pitavastatin.')
q(98, 'Rosuvastatin is annotated as the statin that is:', ['Most commonly used', 'Least used', 'Most potent', 'Longest acting'], 'The response line marks rosuvastatin (m/c used).')
q(98, 'The maximum response dose of rosuvastatin noted on the page is up to:', ['4 mg', '10 mg', '20 mg', '40 mg'], 'The parenthesis says up to 4 mg.')
q(98, 'The ceiling effect of pitavastatin means:', ['Increased dose does not increase response', 'Increased dose increases response linearly', 'Dose must be doubled', 'No response at any dose'], 'The annotation defines ceiling effect: up dose does not up response.')

unit("Statin Side Effects, Contraindications and Clinical Applications in ASCVD",
     "Before starting a statin, baseline CPK and LFT are done although monitoring is not required. Side effects are myopathy (increased with fibrates), hepatotoxicity and hyperglycemia; contraindications are pregnancy and children below 11 years (pravastatin below 8 years), and if a fibrate must be combined, fluvastatin is relatively safer. In ASCVD with raised LDL, an LDL of 100-190 mg/dL plus a 10-year ASCVD risk of 5-20% gets a moderate dose statin while a risk above 20% gets a high dose; diabetes with risk below 20% gets moderate dose and 20% or more gets high dose; and clinical ASCVD gets high dose statins. If response is inadequate, ezetimibe or bile acid binding resins (affordable) or inclisiran can be added.")
q(99, 'The two baseline investigations before starting statins are:', ['CPK and LFT', 'ECG and chest X-ray', 'Urine routine and creatinine', 'TSH and HbA1c'], 'The heading line says do baseline CPK and LFT.')
q(99, 'Regarding monitoring of CPK and LFT on statins, the page notes that it is:', ['Not required', 'Mandatory monthly', 'Required weekly', 'Required only after 1 year'], 'The parenthesis says monitoring not required.')
q(99, 'The risk of myopathy with statins is increased by:', ['Fibrates', 'Ezetimibe', 'Resins', 'Niacin'], 'The S/E line says myopathy: up with fibrates.')
q(99, 'Besides myopathy, the other two statin side effects listed are hepatotoxicity and:', ['Hyperglycemia', 'Hyperkalemia', 'Hypercalcemia', 'Hypoglycemia'], 'The S/E list gives hepatotoxicity and hyperglycemia.')
q(99, 'Statins are contraindicated in pregnancy and in children below:', ['11 years', '8 years', '2 years', '18 years'], 'The C/I line says children <11 years.')
q(99, 'For pravastatin specifically, the contraindicated age is below:', ['8 years', '11 years', '2 years', '16 years'], 'The C/I line says pravastatin: <8 years.')
q(99, 'If a fibrate must be used with a statin, the statin chosen is:', ['Fluvastatin', 'Simvastatin', 'Atorvastatin', 'Lovastatin'], 'The note says if indicated with fibrates use fluvastatin.')
q(99, 'Fluvastatin is preferred with fibrates because of:', ['Relatively lower risk', 'Higher potency', 'Longer half-life', 'Renal excretion'], 'The note says fluvastatin (relatively lower risk).')
q(99, 'In patients with LDL 100-190 mg/dL and a 10-year ASCVD risk of 5-20%, the statin dose is:', ['Moderate dose', 'High dose', 'Low dose', 'No statin'], 'The risk 5-20% branch gives moderate dose statins.')
q(99, 'In the same group, a 10-year ASCVD risk above 20% mandates:', ['High dose statins', 'Moderate dose statins', 'Low dose statins', 'Add-on resin only'], 'The >20% branch gives high dose statins.')
q(99, 'In a diabetic patient with 10-year ASCVD risk below 20%, the statin dose is:', ['Moderate dose', 'High dose', 'No statin', 'Low dose'], 'The DM branch <20% gives moderate dose.')
q(99, 'In a diabetic patient with 10-year ASCVD risk of 20% or more, the statin dose is:', ['High dose', 'Moderate dose', 'Low dose', 'Avoid statin'], 'The DM branch >=20% gives high dose.')
q(99, 'When clinical ASCVD is present, the statin dose is:', ['High dose', 'Moderate dose', 'Low dose', 'None'], 'The clinical ASCVD branch says high dose statins.')
q(99, 'In the dosage table, moderate dose rosuvastatin is:', ['5-10 mg', '20-40 mg', '10-20 mg', '40-80 mg'], 'The table gives rosuvastatin moderate dose 5-10 mg.')
q(99, 'High dose rosuvastatin in the table is:', ['20-40 mg', '5-10 mg', '40-80 mg', '10-20 mg'], 'The table gives rosuvastatin high dose 20-40 mg.')
q(99, 'Moderate dose atorvastatin in the table is:', ['10-20 mg', '5-10 mg', '20-40 mg', '40-80 mg'], 'The table gives atorvastatin moderate dose 10-20 mg.')
q(99, 'High dose atorvastatin in the table is:', ['40-80 mg', '20-40 mg', '10-20 mg', '5-10 mg'], 'The table gives atorvastatin high dose 40-80 mg.')
q(99, 'If the response to statins is inadequate, the agent added to increase effectiveness is:', ['Inclisiran', 'Niacin', 'Fibrate', 'Icosapent'], 'The inadequate response flowchart marks inclisiran as PCSK9 inhibitor, up effectiveness.')
q(99, 'The two affordable add-on drugs listed with bile acid binding resins are ezetimibe and:', ['Bile acid binding resins', 'Fibrates', 'Niacin', 'Icosapent'], 'The flow groups ezetimibe and bile acid binding resins as affordable.')
q(99, 'A caveat about statin therapy from the notes is that low dose statins:', ['Do not prevent mortality', 'Prevent mortality best', 'Cause maximum side effects', 'Are DOC in pregnancy'], 'Note 1 says low dose statins do not prevent mortality.')
q(99, 'According to the notes, which diabetic patients qualify for statin therapy?', ['Every patient of DM', 'Only those with ASCVD', 'Only those above 60 years', 'Only those with LDL >190'], 'Note 2 says every patient of DM is candidate for statin therapy.')

unit("Management of Hypertriglyceridemia and Familial Hypercholesterolemia",
     "Hypertriglyceridemia is managed according to the risk of ASCVD: when risk is present a statin is tried alone, icosapent is added on an inadequate response and fibrates are then added if the response is still inadequate; when ASCVD risk is absent, fibrates are the DOC for the raised TGs and icosapent is added. Familial hypercholesterolemia is treated with high dose statins as the DOC.")
q(100, 'The management of hypertriglyceridemia branches on:', ['Risk of ASCVD', 'Plasma LDL level', 'Age of patient', 'Presence of gout'], 'The HTG flowchart branches from risk of ASCVD present/absent.')
q(100, 'If ASCVD risk is present, the first step in hypertriglyceridemia is:', ['Trial of statins alone', 'Fibrates alone', 'Icosapent alone', 'Resins'], 'The present branch says trial of statins alone.')
q(100, 'For an inadequate response to a statin in hypertriglyceridemia, the next drug added is:', ['Icosapent', 'Fibrates', 'Niacin', 'Ezetimibe'], 'The flow says inadequate response, then add icosapent.')
q(100, 'If the response to icosapent is still inadequate, the drug added is:', ['Fibrates', 'Niacin', 'Resins', 'Bempedoic acid'], 'The final step is inadequate response, then add fibrates.')
q(100, 'If ASCVD risk is absent in hypertriglyceridemia, the DOC is:', ['Fibrates', 'Statins', 'Icosapent', 'Resins'], 'The absent branch says DOC: fibrates, with up TGs.')
q(100, 'In the risk-absent branch, the drug added to fibrates is:', ['Icosapent', 'Niacin', 'Statin', 'Ezetimibe'], 'The absent branch says add icosapent.')
q(100, 'The DOC for familial hypercholesterolemia is:', ['High dose statins', 'Fibrates', 'Bile acid binding resins', 'Icosapent'], 'The FHC section says DOC: high dose statins.')
