start(28)
unit("Types of Epilepsy and Generalised Tonic-Clonic Seizure (GTCS)",
     "Epilepsy is divided into generalised, partial and childhood types. Under generalised come generalised tonic-clonic seizure (GTCS), myoclonic seizure (m.s., whose commonest subtype is juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, JME) and absence seizure (AS); the childhood syndromes are Lennox Gastaut syndrome (LGS) and Dravet syndrome (DS). In GTCS a T-type Ca2+ channel spontaneously generates action potentials, thalamo-cortical reciprocal firing follows, Na+ channels propagate the action potential and the spinal cord depolarises; the features are generalised muscle contraction, teeth clenching, tongue bite with blood from the mouth, and involuntary urination and defecation.")
q(113, 'Epilepsy is classified into generalised, childhood and:', ['Partial', 'Focal only', 'Secondary', 'Cryptogenic only'], 'The epilepsy tree branches into generalised, partial and childhood.')
q(113, 'Under generalised epilepsy, the three seizure types listed are GTCS, myoclonic seizure and:', ['Absence seizure', 'Partial seizure', 'Rolandic epilepsy', 'Dravet syndrome'], 'The generalised branch lists GTCS, m.s. and AS.')
q(113, 'GTCS stands for:', ['Generalised tonic clonic seizure', 'Generalised tonic seizure', 'Grand tonic contraction seizure', 'Generalised tonic-clonic spasm'], 'The tree labels GTCS as generalised tonic clonic seizure.')
q(113, 'The most common subtype of myoclonic seizure is:', ['Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME)', 'Childhood absence epilepsy', 'Rolandic epilepsy', 'Lennox Gastaut syndrome'], 'The myoclonic branch says m/c subtype: juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME).')
q(113, 'The two childhood epileptic syndromes listed are Lennox Gastaut syndrome and:', ['Dravet syndrome', 'West syndrome', 'Ohtahara syndrome', 'Rasmussen syndrome'], 'The childhood branch gives LGS and Dravet syndrome (DS).')
q(113, 'The first step in the GTCS mechanism is spontaneous generation of action potentials by the:', ['T-Type Ca2+ channel', 'Na+ channel', 'K+ channel', 'Cl- channel'], 'The GTCS flow starts with T-type Ca+ channel (spontaneous generation AP).')
q(113, 'T-type Ca2+ channel activity in GTCS leads to:', ['Thalamo-cortical reciprocal firing', 'Spinal cord depolarisation directly', 'Muscle contraction directly', 'Cortical inhibition'], 'The flow says thalamo-cortical reciprocal firing after the T-type channel.')
q(113, 'Propagation of the action potential in GTCS is mediated by:', ['Na+ channel', 'Ca2+ channel', 'K+ channel', 'Cl- channel'], 'The flow labels Na+ channel (propagation of AP).')
q(113, 'The final step of the GTCS mechanism is:', ['Spinal cord depolarization', 'Cortical hyperpolarization', 'Cerebellar inhibition', 'Thalamic inhibition'], 'The flow ends at spinal cord (S.C.) depolarization.')
q(113, 'The brain diagram for GTCS and m.s. labels the sub-cortex as:', ['Thalamus', 'Basal ganglia', 'Hippocampus', 'Midbrain'], 'The diagram labels thalamus (sub cortex).')
q(113, 'In the diagram, the two channels blocked to prevent the action potential of epilepsy are Ca2+ channel and:', ['Na+ channel', 'K+ channel', 'Cl- channel', 'HCN channel'], 'The key shows X Ca2+ channel, X Na+ channel leading to action potential.')
q(113, 'A feature of GTCS is generalised:', ['Muscle contraction', 'Muscle relaxation', 'Focal twitching', 'Sensory aura only'], 'The GTCS features start with generalised m/s contraction.')
q(113, 'Teeth clenching in GTCS is accompanied by:', ['Tongue bite with blood from mouth', 'Lip smacking', 'Drooling only', 'Jaw deviation'], 'The features list teeth clenching and tongue bite (blood from mouth).')
q(113, 'Besides motor features, GTCS is accompanied by involuntary:', ['Urination and defecation', 'Vomiting', 'Coughing', 'Sneezing'], 'The features list involuntary urination and defecation.')

unit("Myoclonic Seizure (m.s.)",
     "Myoclonic seizures occur during the morning and are characterised by twitching of the finger. They are worsened by two antiepileptic drugs - phenytoin and carbamazepine - and their commonest subtype is juvenile myoclonic epilepsy.")
q(113, 'Myoclonic seizures typically occur during the:', ['Morning', 'Night', 'Afternoon', 'Evening only'], 'The m.s. feature says occurs during morning.')
q(113, 'The characteristic feature of myoclonic seizure is:', ['Twitching of finger', 'Generalised tonic contraction', 'Blank stare', 'Tongue bite'], 'The m.s. feature is twitching of finger.')
q(113, 'Myoclonic seizures are worsened by phenytoin and:', ['Carbamazepine', 'Valproate', 'Levetiracetam', 'Clonazepam'], 'The worsened-by line lists phenytoin and carbamazepine.')

unit("Absence Seizure and Partial Seizure",
     "Absence seizure also begins with spontaneous T-type Ca2+ channel activity and then involves Na+ channels. Typical absence seizure (most common) has no motor symptoms but behavioural abnormality with blank stare and surrounding cutoff, and its EEG shows a 3 Hz spike; atypical absence has behavioural changes plus change in muscle tone. Partial seizure, when it generalises, is treated like GTCS. Space occupying lesions (neurocysticercosis, brain tumour, gliosis from trauma or stroke) in the cortex irritate cortical neurons, generate action potentials through Na+ channels that travel to the spinal cord and cause localised depolarisation with localised muscle contraction.")
q(114, 'The absence seizure mechanism begins with spontaneous action potentials from the:', ['T-Type Ca2+ channel', 'Na+ channel', 'K+ channel', 'HCN channel'], 'The AS diagram starts with T-type Ca+ channel (spontaneous AP).')
q(114, 'Typical absence seizure is described as:', ['Most common (m/c)', 'Rare', 'Always atypical', 'Only in adults'], 'The table header says Typical AS (m/c).')
q(114, 'Regarding motor symptoms, typical absence seizure has:', ['No motor symptoms', 'Generalised contraction', 'Focal clonus', 'Tongue bite'], 'The typical AS features say no motor symptoms.')
q(114, 'The behavioural abnormality of typical AS includes blank stare and:', ['Surrounding cutoff', 'Automotor behaviour', 'Déjà vu', 'Fear aura'], 'The typical AS features list blank stare and surrounding cutoff.')
q(114, 'The EEG finding of typical absence seizure is:', ['3 Hz spike', '1 Hz spike', 'Spike and wave at 1 Hz', 'Diffuse slowing'], 'The typical AS row says EEG: 3 Hz spike.')
q(114, 'Atypical absence seizure is characterised by behavioural changes plus:', ['Change in muscle tone', 'Tongue bite', 'Incontinence', 'Generalised convulsion'], 'The atypical AS column says behavioural changes + change in muscle tone.')
q(114, 'When a partial seizure undergoes secondary generalisation, it is treated:', ['Like GTCS', 'Like absence seizure', 'With ketogenic diet', 'With surgery only'], 'The partial seizure flow says a-degree generalization, Rx like GTCS.')
q(114, 'The causes of space occupying lesion (SOL) listed include neurocysticercosis, brain tumour and:', ['Gliosis (neuronal fibrosis)', 'Demyelination', 'Cerebral edema', 'Hydrocephalus'], 'The SOL causes list gives gliosis (neuronal fibrosis).')
q(114, 'Gliosis causing SOL is due to:', ['Trauma or stroke', 'Infection only', 'Genetic causes', 'Radiation only'], 'The gliosis line says d/t trauma and stroke.')
q(114, 'In partial seizure, the SOL lies in the:', ['Cortex', 'Thalamus', 'Brainstem', 'Cerebellum'], 'The flow begins SOL (cortex).')
q(114, 'Cortical SOLs act by irritating:', ['Cortical neurons', 'Thalamic neurons', 'Spinal motor neurons', 'Peripheral nerves'], 'The flow says SOL (cortex) irritates cortical neurons.')
q(114, 'The action potential in partial seizure travels via Na+ channels to the:', ['Spinal cord', 'Cerebellum', 'Brainstem', 'Thalamus'], 'The flow says action potential via Na+ channels then spinal cord.')
q(114, 'The end result of the partial seizure pathway is:', ['Localised depolarisation and localised muscle contraction', 'Generalised contraction', 'Absence', 'Sensory loss'], 'The flow ends at localised depolarization then localised muscle contraction.')

unit("Childhood Epileptic Syndromes",
     "Lennox Gastaut syndrome features resistant seizures and mental retardation; its treatment follows the mnemonic 'very low response to child' - valproate (DOC), lamotrigine (second), rufinamide (third), topiramate, and clobazam/cannabinoids reserved for drop attacks. Dravet syndrome has resistant seizures and is treated with ketogenic diet, valproate (DOC), clonazepam (second) and stiripentol (third). Treatment is continued to a minimum one year seizure-free interval, except in JME, stroke-induced seizure and sleep related hypermotor epilepsy (SHE), where treatment is lifelong.")
q(114, 'The features of Lennox Gastaut syndrome are resistant seizure and:', ['Mental retardation', 'Hemiparesis', 'Visual loss', 'Hearing loss'], 'The LGS row lists resistant seizure and mental retardation.')
q(114, 'The DOC for Lennox Gastaut syndrome is:', ['Valproate', 'Lamotrigine', 'Rufinamide', 'Topiramate'], 'The LGS treatment list gives valproate (DOC).')
q(114, 'The second-line drug for Lennox Gastaut syndrome is:', ['Lamotrigine', 'Rufinamide', 'Topiramate', 'Clobazam'], 'The LGS list marks lamotrigine (2nd).')
q(114, 'The third-line drug for Lennox Gastaut syndrome is:', ['Rufinamide', 'Lamotrigine', 'Topiramate', 'Valproate'], 'The LGS list marks rufinamide (3rd).')
q(114, 'Clobazam/cannabinoids in LGS are reserved for:', ['Drop attacks', 'Status epilepticus', 'Absence seizures', 'Myoclonic jerks'], 'The LGS list says clobazam/cannabinoids reserved for drop attack.')
q(114, 'The dietary treatment for Dravet syndrome is:', ['Ketogenic diet', 'Low protein diet', 'High sodium diet', 'Gluten free diet'], 'The DS row begins with diet: ketogenic.')
q(114, 'The DOC for Dravet syndrome is:', ['Valproate', 'Clonazepam', 'Stiripentol', 'Lamotrigine'], 'The DS list gives valproate (DOC).')
q(114, 'The second-line drug for Dravet syndrome is:', ['Clonazepam', 'Stiripentol', 'Valproate', 'Rufinamide'], 'The DS list marks clonazepam (2nd).')
q(114, 'The third-line drug for Dravet syndrome is:', ['Stiripentol', 'Clonazepam', 'Valproate', 'Topiramate'], 'The DS list marks stiripentol (3rd).')
q(114, 'The usual duration of antiepileptic treatment is a minimum seizure-free interval of:', ['1 year', '2 years', '6 months', '5 years'], 'The note says continue Rx to a minimum a year seizure free interval.')
q(114, 'Which of the following requires lifelong treatment?', ['JME', 'Typical absence', 'Rolandic epilepsy', 'Febrile seizure'], 'The exception list begins with JME.')
q(114, 'Besides JME, lifelong treatment is needed for stroke induced seizure and:', ['Sleep related hypermotor epilepsy (SHE)', 'Rolandic epilepsy', 'LGS', 'DS'], 'The exception list includes SHE.')
unit("Classification of Antiepileptic Drugs",
     "Antiepileptic drugs are classified by mechanism: Na+ channel blockers include phenytoin, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, eslicarbazepine, lamotrigine, topiramate, zonisamide, lacosamide and rufinamide; Ca2+ channel blockers are valproic acid and ethosuximide (DOC in typical absence seizure); other groups are the K+ channel blockers, drugs decreasing glutamate effect, drugs increasing GABA effect and miscellaneous agents.")
q(115, 'Which drug is the DOC for typical absence seizure?', ['Ethosuximide', 'Valproate', 'Lamotrigine', 'Topiramate'], 'The Ca2+ channel blocker branch marks ethosuximide as DOC for typical absence seizure.')
q(115, 'Ethosuximide is grouped as a:', ['Ca2+ channel blocker', 'Na+ channel blocker', 'K+ channel blocker', 'GABA agonist'], 'Ethosuximide sits under Ca2+ channel blocker with valproic acid.')
q(115, 'The other Ca2+ channel blocker in the classification is:', ['Valproic acid', 'Phenytoin', 'Carbamazepine', 'Lacosamide'], 'The Ca2+ channel blocker branch lists valproic acid.')
q(115, 'Which of the following is a Na+ channel blocker?', ['Phenytoin', 'Valproic acid', 'Ethosuximide', 'Stiripentol'], 'Phenytoin heads the Na+ channel blocker list.')
q(115, 'Besides phenytoin and carbamazepine, the Na+ channel blockers include oxcarbazepine and:', ['Eslicarbazepine', 'Ethosuximide', 'Ezogabine', 'Felbamate'], 'The Na+ channel blocker list includes eslicarbazepine.')
q(115, 'Lamotrigine and topiramate are classified as:', ['Na+ channel blockers', 'Ca2+ channel blockers', 'K+ channel blockers', 'GABA analogues'], 'Both appear in the Na+ channel blocker column.')
q(115, 'Which of the following appears in the Na+ channel blocker list?', ['Zonisamide', 'Ethosuximide', 'Vigabatrin', 'Tiagabine'], 'Zonisamide appears in the Na+ channel blocker list.')
q(115, 'Lacosamide and rufinamide belong to which group?', ['Na+ channel blockers', 'Ca2+ channel blockers', 'K+ channel blockers', 'Miscellaneous'], 'Both are listed under Na+ channel blockers.')
q(115, 'The five mechanism groups in the classification include K+ channel blocker, down glutamate effect, up GABA effect, miscellaneous and:', ['Na+ and Ca2+ channel blockers', 'Diuretics', 'Antivirals', 'Anticoagulants'], 'The tree lists Na+ channel blocker and Ca2+ channel blocker along with the other groups.')

unit("Valproic Acid",
     "Valproic acid is a Ca2+ channel blocker with the mechanism mnemonic 'H/o GTCS': it is a histone deacetylase blocker that stops neuronal proliferation, inhibits GABA transaminase so GABA rises, blocks T-type Ca2+ channels and blocks sodium channels. It is used in GTCS, m.s. and atypical absence, and is DOC in rapid cyclers, rheumatic chorea, LGS and DS. Its side effects follow the mnemonic Tab VALPROIC: tremor (epilepsy), maximum teratogenicity (bipolar disorder), vomiting, alopecia, liver toxicity (contraindicated below 2 years), pancreatitis, obesity and increased NH3 with hepatotoxicity, for which L-carnitine is the antidote.")
q(115, 'The mechanism mnemonic for valproic acid is:', ['H/o GTCS', 'Tab VALPROIC', 'GBS', 'DRA VET'], 'The MOA column is headed mnemonic: H/o GTCS.')
q(115, 'As a histone deacetylase blocker, valproic acid acts by:', ['Stopping neuronal proliferation', 'Increasing neuronal proliferation', 'Blocking Na+ channels only', 'Blocking GABA'], 'The MOA line says histone deacetylase blocker (stop neuronal proliferation).')
q(115, 'Valproic acid inhibits GABA transaminase, which leads to:', ['Increased GABA', 'Decreased GABA', 'Increased glutamate', 'Decreased glycine'], 'The MOA line says GABA transaminase inhibition, then up GABA.')
q(115, 'The channel blocked by valproic acid in the MOA list is the:', ['T-Type Ca2+ channel', 'L-type Ca2+ channel', 'K+ channel', 'HCN channel'], 'The MOA list includes T-type Ca2+ channel blockers.')
q(115, 'Valproic acid also blocks:', ['Sodium channels', 'Potassium channels', 'Chloride channels', 'Aquaporins'], 'The MOA list ends with sodium channel blockers.')
q(115, 'Valproic acid is used in GTCS, m.s. and:', ['Atypical A.S.', 'Typical A.S. only', 'Partial seizure only', 'Status epilepticus'], 'The uses list gives GTCS, m.s., atypical A.S.')
q(115, 'Valproic acid is DOC in rapid cyclers and:', ['Rheumatic chorea', 'Rolandic epilepsy', 'Trigeminal neuralgia', 'Infantile spasm'], 'The DOC list includes rapid cyclers and rheumatic chorea.')
q(115, 'The two childhood syndromes for which valproic acid is DOC are DS and:', ['LGS', 'SHE', 'JME only', 'Rolandic epilepsy'], 'The DOC list ends with LGS and DS.')
q(115, 'In the side-effect mnemonic Tab VALPROIC, T stands for tremor and teratogenic with:', ['Maximum teratogenicity', 'Minimum teratogenicity', 'No teratogenicity', 'Teratogenicity only in males'], 'The mnemonic line says teratogenic (max).')
q(115, 'The side effect V in Tab VALPROIC is:', ['Vomiting', 'Vertigo', 'Vision loss', 'Vitiligo'], 'The mnemonic lists vomiting.')
q(115, 'The side effect A in Tab VALPROIC is:', ['Alopecia', 'Anemia', 'Ataxia', 'Agranulocytosis'], 'The mnemonic lists alopecia.')
q(115, 'Valproate is contraindicated in children below:', ['2 years', '5 years', '11 years', '8 years'], 'The liver toxicity line says C/I in child < 2 yrs.')
q(115, 'The pancreatic side effect of valproate in the mnemonic is:', ['Pancreatitis', 'Pancreatic cancer', 'Diabetes mellitus', 'Steatorrhea'], 'The mnemonic lists pancreatitis.')
q(115, 'The metabolic side effect listed for valproate is:', ['Obesity', 'Weight loss', 'Metabolic acidosis', 'Hyperkalemia'], 'The mnemonic lists obesity.')
q(115, 'Valproate increases NH3 and is hepatotoxic; the antidote for this toxicity is:', ['L-Carnitine', 'N-acetylcysteine', 'Flumazenil', 'Atropine'], 'The line says increased NH3 (hepatotoxic) with antidote L-carnitine.')

unit("Phenytoin (and Fosphenytoin)",
     "Phenytoin (diphenyl hydantoin) is used orally for GTCS and PS and intravenously for status epilepticus, with purple glove syndrome as a side effect of the IV route. Its side effects follow the mnemonic HYDANTOIN: hirsutism, hyperplastic gum, hyperglycemia, lymphadenopathy resembling Hodgkin lymphoma, and (as acute toxicity) diplopia, ataxia and nystagmus; it is teratogenic (facial cleft) and causes osteomalacia (treat with vitamin D and vitamin K), increased bleeding in newborns (give injectable vitamin K after birth) and neutropenia/anemia from folic acid depletion. Fosphenytoin is water soluble with less toxicity and is preferred over phenytoin.")
q(116, 'The oral uses of phenytoin are GTCS and:', ['PS (partial seizure)', 'Absence seizure', 'Myoclonic seizure', 'Infantile spasm'], 'The oral uses line gives GTCS and PS.')
q(116, 'The IV use of phenytoin is:', ['Status epilepticus (S.E.)', 'Absence seizure', 'Prophylaxis of febrile seizure', 'Migraine'], 'The IV line says status epilepticus (S.E.).')
q(116, 'A side effect specifically linked to the IV route of phenytoin is:', ['Purple glove syndrome', 'Blue pigmentation', 'Grey baby syndrome', 'Red man syndrome'], 'The side effect line says purple glove syndrome.')
q(116, 'The side-effect mnemonic for phenytoin is:', ['HYDANTOIN', 'Tab VALPROIC', 'GBS', 'VET'], 'The S/E column is headed mnemonic HYDANTOIN.')
q(116, 'In the phenytoin mnemonic, H stands for hirsutism and:', ['Hyperplastic gum', 'Hyperglycemia only', 'Hyperkalemia', 'Hypotension'], 'The mnemonic starts with hirsutism and hyperplastic gum.')
q(116, 'Phenytoin causes hyperglycemia and:', ['Lymphadenopathy resembling Hodgkin lymphoma', 'Non-Hodgkin lymphoma', 'Leukemia', 'Multiple myeloma'], 'The mnemonic line says lymphadenopathy (resembles Hodgkins lymphoma).')
q(116, 'The acute toxicity features of phenytoin are diplopia, ataxia and:', ['Nystagmus', 'Papilledema', 'Optic atrophy', 'Ptosis'], 'The acute toxicity bracket groups diplopia, ataxia and nystagmus.')
q(116, 'The teratogenic effect of phenytoin is:', ['Facial cleft', 'Neural tube defect', 'Limb reduction', 'Cardiac septal defect'], 'The mnemonic says teratogenic: facial cleft.')
q(116, 'Phenytoin causes osteomalacia, which is treated with vitamin D and:', ['vitamin K', 'vitamin A', 'vitamin C', 'vitamin B12'], 'The line says osteomalacia (Rx vit D and vit K).')
q(116, 'Increased bleeding in newborns of mothers on phenytoin is treated with:', ['Inj. vitamin K after birth', 'Inj. vitamin D after birth', 'Platelet transfusion', 'Fresh frozen plasma'], 'The line says increased bleeding in newborns (Rx inj. vit K after birth).')
q(116, 'The hematological side effects of phenytoin are neutropenia and anemia due to decreased:', ['Folic acid', 'Vitamin B12', 'Iron', 'Copper'], 'The mnemonic ends with neutropenia, anemia (d/t down folic acid).')
q(116, 'Fosphenytoin is preferred over phenytoin because it is:', ['Water soluble with less toxicity', 'Lipid soluble with more toxicity', 'Given orally only', 'Longer acting'], 'The fosphenytoin line says water soluble (less toxicity), preferred over phenytoin.')

unit("Carbamazepine",
     "Carbamazepine is used in GTCS, PS (where it was the older DOC) and SHE. It is DOC in trigeminal neuralgia and in Rolandic epilepsy, which occurs in childhood during sleep or after awakening and is treated for 1-2 years. Its side effects are hyponatremia with SIADH (a delayed effect, most common in the elderly), hypersensitivity with eosinophilia, aplastic anemia, agranulocytosis and Stevens Johnson syndrome, plus ataxia and diplopia/blurred vision.")
q(116, 'The uses of carbamazepine are GTCS, SHE and:', ['PS (older DOC)', 'Absence seizure', 'Infantile spasm', 'Atypical AS'], 'The uses list gives GTCS, PS (older DOC) and SHE.')
q(116, 'Carbamazepine is DOC in trigeminal neuralgia and:', ['Rolandic epilepsy', 'Lennox Gastaut syndrome', 'Dravet syndrome', 'West syndrome'], 'The DOC list gives trigeminal neuralgia and Rolandic epilepsy.')
q(116, 'Rolandic epilepsy occurs in:', ['Childhood', 'Elderly', 'Neonates only', 'Adults'], 'The Rolandic epilepsy occurrence line says childhood.')
q(116, 'Rolandic epilepsy seizures occur during sleep or:', ['After awakening', 'During meals', 'During exercise', 'On exposure to light'], 'The occurrence line says during sleep or after awakening.')
q(116, 'Rolandic epilepsy is treated for:', ['1-2 years', '1-2 months', 'Lifelong', '6 months'], 'The Rx line says for 1-2 years.')
q(116, 'Carbamazepine causes hyponatremia through:', ['SIADH', 'Diabetes insipidus', 'Cerebral salt wasting', 'Renal tubular acidosis'], 'The S/E line says hyponatremia to SIADH.')
q(116, 'The SIADH effect of carbamazepine is described as:', ['Delayed S/E', 'Immediate S/E', 'Dose-independent', 'Rare in elderly'], 'The SIADH line is annotated delayed S/E.')
q(116, 'Carbamazepine-induced SIADH is most common in which population?', ['Elderly', 'Children', 'Pregnant women', 'Neonates'], 'The line says m/c elderly population.')
q(116, 'The hypersensitivity reaction of carbamazepine includes eosinophilia and:', ['Aplastic anemia', 'Megaloblastic anemia', 'Iron deficiency anemia', 'Hemolytic anemia'], 'The hypersensitivity list includes aplastic anemia.')
q(116, 'Besides aplastic anemia, carbamazepine hypersensitivity causes agranulocytosis and:', ['SJS (Stevens Johnson Syndrome)', 'DRESS only', 'TEN only', 'Erythema nodosum'], 'The list includes SJS.')
q(116, 'The neurological side effects of carbamazepine are ataxia and:', ['Diplopia/blurred vision', 'Nystagmus only', 'Tremor', 'Aphasia'], 'The S/E list ends with ataxia and diplopia/blurred vision.')
unit("Lamotrigine and Topiramate",
     "Lamotrigine inhibits glutamate release and blocks Na+/Ca2+ channels, with an additional DHFR/5HT3 blocking action; it is used in GTCS, m.s., PS, the depressive phase of bipolar disorder and LGS. Its side effect is Stevens Johnson syndrome, so the drug must be started gradually, and it is contraindicated in acute mania where a bolus dose is needed. Topiramate acts through GPCR-mediated increased GABAA release, decreased glutamate release, potassium channel opening, Ca2+/carbonic anhydrase inhibition and resting Na channel blockade; it shares the uses of lamotrigine and is additionally used for obesity with phentermine (an anorexic agent) and for migraine prophylaxis. Its side effects are renal stones, metabolic acidosis, weight loss and angle closure glaucoma.")
q(117, 'Lamotrigine inhibits the release of:', ['Glutamate', 'GABA', 'Acetylcholine', 'Dopamine'], 'The lamotrigine antiepileptic action says inhibits glutamate release.')
q(117, 'Besides glutamate release, lamotrigine acts as a:', ['Na+/Ca2+ channel blocker', 'K+ channel opener', 'Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor', 'DHFR activator'], 'The lamotrigine line says Na+/Ca2+ channel blocker.')
q(117, 'The other action of lamotrigine is blockade of:', ['DHFR/5HT3', 'COX-2', 'PDE5', 'HMG-CoA'], 'The lamotrigine other action is DHFR/5HT3 blocker.')
q(117, 'Lamotrigine is used in GTCS, m.s., PS and:', ['Bipolar disorder (depressive phase)', 'Bipolar mania', 'Schizophrenia', 'Anxiety disorder'], 'The uses list gives bipolar disorder (depressive phase).')
q(117, 'The childhood syndrome for which lamotrigine is used is:', ['LGS', 'DS', 'West syndrome', 'SHE'], 'The uses list ends with LGS.')
q(117, 'The major side effect of lamotrigine is:', ['SJS', 'SLE', 'DRESS only', 'TEN only'], 'The S/E line says SJS.')
q(117, 'Because of SJS, lamotrigine must be:', ['Started gradually', 'Started with a bolus', 'Given IV', 'Given with a steroid'], 'The S/E line says SJs: start drug gradually.')
q(117, 'Lamotrigine is contraindicated in acute mania because:', ['A bolus dose is needed', 'It causes SJS immediately', 'It causes renal stones', 'It worsens seizures'], 'The C/I line says acute mania (bolus dose needed).')
q(117, 'Topiramate MOA works through:', ['GPCR', 'Nuclear receptor', 'Ion channel only', 'Enzyme inhibition only'], 'The topiramate MOA line says GPCR.')
q(117, 'Topiramate increases the release of:', ['GABAA', 'Glutamate', 'Acetylcholine', 'Noradrenaline'], 'The MOA says up GABAA release.')
q(117, 'Along with increasing GABAA release, topiramate decreases release of:', ['Glutamate', 'GABA', 'Glycine', 'Histamine'], 'The MOA says down glutamate release.')
q(117, 'Topiramate acts as an opener of:', ['Potassium channels', 'Sodium channels', 'Calcium channels only', 'Chloride channels'], 'The MOA says potassium channel opener.')
q(117, 'Topiramate inhibits carbonic anhydrase along with which channel?', ['Ca2+ channel', 'Na+ channel only', 'K+ channel', 'Cl- channel'], 'The MOA says Ca2+/carbonic anhydrase inhibitor.')
q(117, 'The channel blocker action of topiramate is described as:', ['Resting Na channel blocker', 'Open Na channel blocker', 'Late inward Na blocker', 'Delayed rectifier K blocker'], 'The MOA ends with resting Na channel blocker.')
q(117, 'Besides the uses shared with lamotrigine, topiramate is used in obesity with:', ['Phentermine (anorexic agent)', 'Orlistat', 'Liraglutide', 'Bupropion'], 'The other uses line says obesity used with phentermine (anorexic agent).')
q(117, 'The other additional use of topiramate is:', ['Migraine prophylaxis', 'Migraine abortion', 'Cluster headache', 'Tension headache only'], 'The other uses list ends with migraine prophylaxis.')
q(117, 'The renal side effect of topiramate is:', ['Renal stones', 'Renal failure', 'Nephrotic syndrome', 'Dialysis disequilibrium'], 'The S/E list gives renal stones.')
q(117, 'The metabolic side effect of topiramate is metabolic acidosis, which is also seen with:', ['CA inhibitors', 'Loop diuretics', 'Thiazides', 'Mannitol'], 'The bracket says metabolic acidosis is a CA inhibitor S/E.')
q(117, 'Topiramate causes weight loss and:', ['Angle closure glaucoma', 'Open angle glaucoma', 'Cataract', 'Optic neuritis'], 'The S/E list gives angle closure glaucoma.')
q(117, 'Before starting topiramate, the examination advised is:', ['Ophthalmic examination', 'Cardiac evaluation', 'Renal biopsy', 'Hearing test'], 'The S/E line says angle closure glaucoma (ophthalmic examination done).')

unit("Other Na+ Channel Blockers: Oxcarbazepine, Zonisamide, Lacosamide and Rufinamide",
     "Oxcarbazepine and eslicarbazepine are prodrugs with less hypersensitivity but more hyponatremia, so they should be avoided in the elderly. Zonisamide causes renal stones, lacosamide increases suicidal tendencies and rufinamide is used in LGS. The page notes drugs associated with SJS and HLA: carbamazepine with HLA B1502, abacavir with HLA B5701 and allopurinol with HLA B5801.")
